{
  "summary": "Restructures peer connection order and ping timings in the `p2p_eviction.py` functional test so that peer eviction protection for block and transaction relayers is genuinely exercised. Eviction candidate peers are now connected first and ping faster than tx and block peers, making test assertions independent of platform-specific `std::sort` behavior in netgroup protection. Also adds checks that `last_transaction` and `last_block` are recorded in `getpeerinfo()` and waits for min ping reporting.",
  "problem": "The `p2p_eviction.py` test passed even when block-time protection was disabled or bypassed entirely, because block-sending peers were connected first and fell back to connection-age protection. Additionally, because all mock peers share a single netgroup, netgroup protection's peer selection depended on C++ standard library sorting implementations (such as libc++ vs libstdc++ vs MSVC).",
  "evidence": [
    "PR description demonstrates that removing peer block relay (`block_peer.send_blocks_and_test([block], node, success=True)`) still resulted in a passing test on master.",
    "Commit message notes that connecting block peers last was insufficient because netgroup sorting with libc++ and MSVC insertion sort retains the youngest peers."
  ],
